eset PC at $8057 (the reset routine's final JMP target) plus the title screen is a nice confirmation.\n\nActually, I can do better: after reset, immediately use trace_writes? No.\n\nAlternatively use the fact that at the moment of reset, the stack pointer, etc. Not needed.\n\nLet me also verify the main loop: $8057 is JMP $8057 — infinite wait loop; the game does everything in NMI (all the game logic is called from NMI at $80ea JSR $8182 etc.). That's SMB's architecture: main thread just waits, NMI does everything. Good detail.\n\nNow also I should use search_memory as the task asks (toolbox). Could search PRG for JSR/JMP to $8082 or references. Not needed. But let me quickly check: is there any JMP/JSR to $8082? No, only vector. Let's search for the vector bytes? Eh.\n\nI think evidence is solid. Let me also record the routine lengths:\n- Reset: $8000–$8059, length 0x5A = 90 bytes (entry to the JMP at $8057 which is 3 bytes, ends $805A). The self-loop is $8057: 4C 57 80. So routine length 0x5A (90 bytes) including the loop. Actually the loop is part of the main loop, not reset. Reset routine ends when it transfers to $8057. Length from $8000 to $8057 = 0x57 = 87 bytes. I'll say length 0x57 (87 bytes) to the main-loop wait at $8057.\n- NMI: $8082–$8181 RTI, length 0x100 = 256 bytes.\n\nNow record mappings. Two routines. Notes must include how found and the instructions that do the job, source 'trace and disassembly'.\n\nFor reset: found by reading the RESET vector at $FFFC/$FFFD = $00 $80 → $8000. Instructions: SEI ($8000) / CLD ($8001) disable IRQs and decimal mode; LDA #$10 / STA $2000 ($8002-$8004) sets PPUCTRL with NMI off during init; LDX #$FF / TXS ($8007-$8009) initializes the stack; polls PPU status $2002 twice ($800A-$8013) to wait two vblanks for PPU warm-up; checks battery/continue marker $07FF==$A5; clears APU $4015 and sets PPUMASK; JSR $8220/$8E19 init; INC $0774; then JMP $8057 (self-loop) where the CPU idles and all work happens in NMI.\n\nProof: res