{"type":"text","text":"Done. Both interrupt-routine entries are found, proven, and promoted (`promote_mappings` saved them into the game's mapping file).\n\n## Vectors read from the last six bytes of CPU space (`read_memory $FFFA`, 6 bytes → `82 80 00 80 F0 FF`)\n\n| vector | bytes | target |\n|---|---|---|\n| NMI | `$FFFA/$FFFB` = `$82 $80` | **$8082** |\n| RESET | `$FFFC/$FFFD` = `$00 $80` | **$8000** |\n| IRQ/BRK | `$FFFE/$FFFF` = `$F0 $FF` | $FFF0 |\n\n## Reset routine — entry **$8000** (87 bytes, `$8000–$8056`)\n\nWhat it does (disassembly at `$8000`):\n\n- `$8000 SEI` / `$8001 CLD` — mask interrupts, clear decimal mode\n- `$8002 LDA #$10` / `$8004 STA $2000` — PPUCTRL with NMI off during init\n- `$8007 LDX #$FF` / `$8009 TXS` — stack to `$01FF`\n- `$800A–$8013 LDA $2002` / `BPL` twice — poll PPUSTATUS to wait two vblanks (PPU warm-up)\n- `$8014–$8029` — check the continue marker `$07FF == $A5` and pick the boot state via `$90CC`\n- `$802E STA $4011`, `$8031 STA $0770`, `$8036/$8039` write the resume markers `$07FF=$A5`, `$07A7=$A5`\n- `$803E LDA #$0F` / `STA $4015` — APU enable; `$8043 LDA #$06` / `STA $2001` — PPUMASK, both layers on\n- `$8046 JSR $8220`, `$8049 JSR $8E19` — init; `$804C INC $0774`; `$804F–$8054` enable NMI\n- `$8057 JMP $8057` — the routine hands control to an idle wait loop\n\n**Evidence:** the RESET vector is the only entry (`$FFFC`), and `reset_emulator` followed by `get_cpu_state` returned **PC `$8057`** with a fresh cycle counter and the title screen restored — the CPU had run the code at `$8000` and landed exactly on its own `JMP $8057` target. The main thread then never leaves that loop; all work happens in the NMI.\n\n## NMI handler — entry **$8082** (256 bytes, `$8082–$8181`, ends `RTI`)\n\nWhat it does (disassembly at `$8082`):\n\n- `$8082–$808C` — read `$0778`, mask and store, then `STA $2000` (PPUCTRL: nametable/sprite size, NMI bit cleared while setting up)\n- `$80A3 STA $2001` (PPUMASK from `$0779`); `$80A6 LDA $2002` (clear the vblank latch)\n- `$80B3 LDA #$02 / STA $4014` — **OAM DMA** (page 2 → hardware sprites), the hallmark of the NMI\n- `$80B6–$80C3` — index tables `$805A`/`$806D` by the VRAM-queue state `$0773` and `JSR $8EDD` (queue transfer)\n- `$80E4–$80ED` — `JSR $F2D0`, `JSR $8E5C`, `JSR $8182`, `JSR $8F97` (per-frame game logic)\n- `$80F0–$8138` — frame timers (`$0747`, `$077F`, `$0780,X`) and controller shifting\n- `$815C/$8165 STA $2005` — PPUSCROLL x then y; `$8178 ORA #$80 / STA $2000` re-enables NMI; `$8181 RTI`\n\n**Evidence:** `trace_writes` on CPU `$2000` for 60 frames returned **60 writes from PC `$808F`, 60 from `$816F`, 60 from `$8181`** — a full set of PPUCTRL writes every single frame — and all three write PCs lie inside `$8082–$8181`. Since the main thread idles at `$8057`, every frame's hardware work is this handler.\n\nBoth recorded with `record_mapping` (routine, confidence 1, source \"trace and disassembly\") and promoted: the mapping file now holds the two entries alongside the previously mapped routines. The emulator is running again."}
